course: dictionary
lesson: 8882
title: Shovel: a tool and an action
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can be a noun, which names a thing, or a verb, which names an action. Shovel is both. As a noun, a shovel is a tool with a long handle and a wide, slightly curved blade. «Long ago tunnels were dug by hand with picks, shovels and gunpowder.» Here shovels are things that workers hold.
explain: As a verb, shovel means to lift and move something with a shovel. «A fireman shovelled coal into the firebox» tells you what he did. The clues are coal and firebox: he was moving coal, not eating it or painting it.
explain: Shovel has an informal meaning too, to put a lot of something somewhere quickly and roughly. «He shovelled food into his mouth» has no spade in it. The clues are food and mouth, so it means he ate fast and roughly. To check, swap in your meaning: "He lifted food into his mouth with a shovel" makes no sense, so this must be the informal meaning.
fact: A shovel is a tool with a long handle and a wide, slightly curved blade.
fact: Shovel can be a noun (a shovel) or a verb (to shovel).
fact: «Shovelled» is the British spelling, and «shoveled» is the American spelling. Both are correct.
fact: In Canada and the northern USA, people often shovel snow from paths in winter, so a snow shovel is a very common tool.
fact: «Shovel it in» is an informal phrase that means to eat quickly and in large amounts.
exercise: «A steam engine had a fireman who shovelled coal into the firebox.» What does shovelled mean here? | Waved a flag to start the train | *Lifted and moved coal with a shovel | Painted the engine red | Blew a loud whistle
exercise: «He shovelled food into his mouth.» What does shovelled suggest about how he ate? | With a knife and fork | Only a little at a time | *Quickly and roughly | Slowly and politely
exercise: Find the word in «Long ago tunnels were dug by hand with picks, shovels and gunpowder» that names a tool a worker can hold. | dug | Long | *shovels | tunnels
test:
Nadia says shovel can only be a thing. Is she right? | *No, it can also be an action, as in shovel snow | Yes, it is always a tool | Yes, it is always a verb | No, it is a colour
Ella reads «shovel snow». What does it mean? | To sweep snow away with a broom | *To lift and move snow with a shovel | To eat snow quickly | To melt snow with heat
exam:
Jayden writes, «They shovelled the sand onto the beach.» What does shovelled mean? | *Lifted and moved sand with a shovel | Built a sandcastle with a bucket | Walked slowly across the beach | Swam out into the sea
